in the midst of or just after their  dramatic release of the State Department  cables I got a call from mark Schmucker  former co-chairman of universal and he  said would you like to do this and I  left at it I mean it came in an  inconvenient time I was doing some other  stuff but I thought I can I can't not do  this it's such an important story and at  the time I thought it was about two  things I thought it was a simple David  and Goliath story meaning Julian Assange  against the US government and I thought  it was about a leaking machine this  electronic dropbox and that was going to  be very interesting it ended up being  something very different I mean Julian's  character changed i discovered the  character bradley manning threw out it  became a much more important figure and  also rather than being about a leaking  machine and ended up being a story about  human relationships in the time of the  internet and that you know was also a  very interesting revelation so it you  know the the scope of it changed very  much as we were pursuing the story so as  Bradley story started to discover that  how did you go about packaging it all  together well we wanted the back and  forth between Bradley and Julian the  problem we had I mean in addition to the  fact that the Julian didn't want to talk  to me or at least he didn't want to do  an on-camera interview I had a character  who was in prison and the United States  government had no interest in letting  the atom so how would I present Bradley  Manning's character I mean not going to  talk to other people about him but in  the course of making the film another  batch of these initial chats with  Adrienne mama were released by Wired  magazine and when we saw that second  batch sort of the full portrait of  Bradley's character came through and we  thought we'll wait a minute maybe we can  rather than getting an actor to read  them or whatever we can actually show  them as he saw them as Adrian lamo saw  them that is to say to print them and  that led us down a road of trying to  build into the film a whole sort of  graph  component but also to have this a  character that would exist primarily  through the chats that he wrote down on  his computer that seemed to be a very  intriguing idea and we were terrified at  first as the whether or not this would  work but over time we got the feeling  that actually it was getting at a kind  of intimacy and it's a funny kind of  intimacy because it's at once very  isolating and intimate all at the same  time and of course he was communicating  with a complete stranger I got involved  early on I suspected that because so  much of this story would be about the  internet and it became much more about  the Internet in a way not only about  secrets what should be secret which  should not be secret the surveillance  state and all of that but also about how  we're all being changed by the internet  so I wanted to somehow find a way to to  make that come alive on screen and I  formed a partnership with this company  called frame store which is a you know  very well known graphics company they do  a lot of title sequences that had the  special effects were harry potter and  the new york office in particular wanted  to become involved at an early stage to  be able to get involved in the story  from the beginning so that you know very  often when i work with a graphics  company they come in at the end and you  go through this process where they  always show you beautiful stuff but it's  very often inappropriate for the story  so they were involved with a story from  the beginning and so particularly with  these chats we work very long and hard  to try to give them a character that  wasn't just printed characters on a  screen but they they felt like they were  emanating from the screen and there was  something both handmade and computer ish  about them that would give the character  of these two characters who communicate  only by text you know a sense of  humanity do you think that if you had a  sit-down interview with Julian on screen  with that how do you think that would  have changed the story if at all I was  given this assignment at a time when  Julian had become an incredibly famous  person and by the time I approached him  and one of my executive producers was  one of his was one of the people  actually put up some of the money for  his bail so I was plugged right in to  his camp  but he was already surrounded by agents  and lawyers and hangers-on and political  activists so he had become a kind of  human commodity the more I learned about  Julian and I did meet him a number of  times but a more I learned about through  other people the more convinced I wanted  to find out and find a way to portray  him prior to when he became famous and I  established a relationship with this  wonderful Australian filmmaker Mark  Davis who had followed him with just  himself in a camera and a little mic on  the camera and gotten all this wonderful  footage of Julian before he was famous  and that gives a flavor of the character  of a more idealistic person who's who's  who's in search of a dream but hasn't  yet found it
 

